.TH SET_SUB 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_sub \- Subtract one set from another and store the result

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB int
.IB set_sub(const set_t* a, const set_t* b, set_t* c);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_sub\fR function subtracts one set from another and stores the result. It takes three arguments: the set to subtract from, the set to subtract, and a set to store the result. The function returns the number of elements in the result.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB a
The set to subtract from.
.TP
.IB b
The set to subtract.
.TP
.IB c
A set to store the result.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns the number of elements in the result.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_sub\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set_a, set_b, set_c;
    set_init(&set_a);
    set_init(&set_b);
    set_init(&set_c);

    set_inserts(&set_a, "apple");
    set_inserts(&set_a, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set_a, "cherry");

    set_inserts(&set_b, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set_b, "cherry");
    set_inserts(&set_b, "date");

    printf("Set A: %u elements\n", set.len);
    printf("Set B: %u elements\n", set_b.len);

    int r = set_sub(&set_a, &set_b, &set_c);

    printf("Set C (result of A - B): %u elements\n", set_c.len);

    set_free(&set_a);
    set_free(&set_b);
    set_free(&set_c);
    return 0;
}
.nef